{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean-Marie","familyName":"Barbaroux","name":"Barbaroux, Jean-Marie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Horia D.","familyName":"Cornean","name":"Cornean, Horia D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Loïc Le","familyName":"Treust","name":"Treust, Loïc Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Raymond","name":"Raymond, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edgardo","familyName":"Stockmeyer","name":"Stockmeyer, Edgardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Green's functions for magnetic Dirac operators and bulk-edge correspondence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35Pxx, 81Qxx, 81Q10, 35Q40, 35P05, 35J08","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T16:48:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:59:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-14T09:13:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-15T00:58:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s00023-026-01759-z","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the resolvent kernel (Green's function) of magnetic Dirac operators on a half-plane with boundary conditions interpolating between infinite mass and zigzag cases, excluding the latter. We show that these kernels have all the required properties so that the proof of bulk-edge correspondence from the infinite mass case shown recently can be repeated ad litteram. The zigzag case exhibits qualitatively different behavior and will be addressed in future work."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:05:14Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:05:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-15T03:09:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16306","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16306","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16306","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["MPP"],"givenName":"Georg G.","familyName":"Raffelt","name":"Raffelt, Georg G.","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["MPA"],"givenName":"Hans-Thomas","familyName":"Janka","name":"Janka, Hans-Thomas","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["DESY Zeuthen"],"givenName":"Damiano F. G.","familyName":"Fiorillo","name":"Fiorillo, Damiano F. G.","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Neutrinos from core-collapse supernovae"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-19T18:00:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:02:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-28T16:14:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-25T00:49:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/b978-0-443-26598-3.00065-1","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The core of a massive star (M \u0026gt; 8 Msun) eventually collapses. This implosion usually triggers a supernova (SN) explosion that ejects most of the stellar envelope and leaves behind a neutron star (NS) with a mass of up to about 2 Msun. Sometimes the explosion fails and a black hole forms instead. The NS radiates its immense binding energy (some 10% of its rest mass or $2-4\\times10^{53}$ erg) almost entirely as neutrinos and antineutrinos of all flavors with typical energies of some 10 MeV. This makes core-collapse SNe the most powerful neutrino factories in the Universe. Such a signal was observed once - with limited statistics - from SN 1987A in the Large Magellanic Cloud. Today, however, many large neutrino detectors act as SN observatories and would register a high-statistics signal. A future Galactic SN, though rare (1-3 per century), would produce a wealth of astrophysical and particle-physics information, including possible signatures for new particles. Neutrinos are key to SN dynamics in the framework of the Bethe-Wilson delayed explosion paradigm. After collapse, they are trapped in the core for a few seconds, forming a dense neutrino plasma that can exhibit collective flavor evolution caused by the weak interaction, a subject of intense theoretical research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To be published in the Encyclopedia of Particle Physics (in a different format), final version with a few additional references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16306","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:45:20Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:45:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-25T02:51:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18088","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18088","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18088","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuhao","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Chuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evangelos","familyName":"Pournaras","name":"Pournaras, Evangelos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimization without Future Compromises? Decentralized Coordination via Collective and Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:58:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:50:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-23T14:40:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-24T01:08:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient resource allocation in multi-agent systems requires autonomous agents to coordinate their decisions while balancing system-wide objectives with individual costs. This becomes increasingly challenging over long time horizons, where decisions that improve the current allocation may compromise future resource allocation, while decentralized agents have limited observations of the overall system. Multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) can learn such long-term dependencies via local observations, but directly applying it to large-scale coordination leads to rapidly growing decision spaces and inefficient training. To this end, we propose Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning (HRCL), a hierarchical framework that uses MARL to guide, rather than replace, decentralized multi-agent coordination. At the high level, MARL learns strategies that restrict the alternatives considered during coordination and guide agents in balancing system-wide and individual objectives. At the low level, agents perform efficient decentralized coordination under this strategic guidance. This separation reduces the learning space and allows short-term coordination trade-offs to be evaluated according to their long-term effects. Experiments on a synthetic benchmark show that HRCL converges substantially faster than standalone MARL and reduces system-wide and individual costs by 35.53% and 27.05%, respectively. Evaluations on energy self-management and drone swarm sensing further show improved resource allocation, power-peak regulation, and sensing efficiency. These results show that learning strategic guidance for an existing coordination process can retain scalable decentralized coordination without letting short-term decisions compromise future resource allocation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18088","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:27:18Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:27:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-24T02:49:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18016","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18016","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18016","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Crowe","name":"Crowe, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Evans","name":"Evans, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexei","familyName":"Smolyaninov","name":"Smolyaninov, Alexei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Analysis of polymerized superconducting circuits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:56:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-23T00:23:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/1361-6668/ae28e6","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We apply polymer quantization, a quantization technique sometimes used in high energy physics, to several superconducting circuits including: transmons, transmission line resonators, and LC circuits. In the case of transmon qubits and transmission line resonators, experimental predictions are very close to what is found with canonical quantization, though in this approach constant charge offsets can be interpreted as quantization ambiguities. In the case of LC circuits, polymer quantization predicts nonlinearities which are not present in the canonical approach. Based on this analysis we design and analyze a qubit which uses a meander inductor instead of a Josephson junction. Implications for qubit performance and fabrication are discussed. Given a choice for an effective phase operator, relevant parameters such as anharmonicity, frequency, and dispersive shifts are calculated for this meander inductor based qubit."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18016","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:25:38Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:25:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-23T02:46:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17123","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17123","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17123","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Manly","name":"Manly, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mitchell","familyName":"Lahare","name":"Lahare, Mitchell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hilary","familyName":"Utaegbulam","name":"Utaegbulam, Hilary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Last","name":"Last, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Facilitating neutron and energy reconstruction in neutrino events using the direction of tagged neutrons"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T15:27:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:57:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-20T16:59:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-21T01:49:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-14T20:02:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-16T00:09:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To date, accelerator neutrino experiments have had only limited success including neutrons in the neutrino event reconstruction event by event. Produced neutrons often interact in a detector leaving an energy deposition that does not correlate strongly with the kinetic energy of the neutron. This work explores the inclusion of the direction of a neutron tagged by such an energy deposition in concert with the missing transverse momentum of the reconstructed particles to determine the approximate kinetic energy of the neutron and modify the reconstruction of the energy of the incoming neutrino. The technique significantly increases the neutron kinetic energy estimation relative to one that assigns the neutron kinetic energy by enforcing transverse momentum balance alone. When included in the neutrino energy calculation, the neutrino energy resolution is improved and the reconstructed energy is distributed more symmetrically around the true value. The technique shows promise and might be used to good effect to analyze data taken with experiments able to tag neutron energy deposits with good neutron direction resolution such as the T2K near detector and the liquid argon detectors in the SBN program and DUNE."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17123","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:04:34Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:04:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-16T02:40:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16248","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16248","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16248","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Savini","familyName":"Kashmira","name":"Kashmira, Savini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jayanaka","familyName":"Dantanarayana","name":"Dantanarayana, Jayanaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thamirawaran","familyName":"Sathiyalogeswaran","name":"Sathiyalogeswaran, Thamirawaran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krisztian","familyName":"Flautner","name":"Flautner, Krisztian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjia","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Lingjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Mars","name":"Mars, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GraphMend: Code Transformations for Fixing Graph Breaks in PyTorch 2"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-17T17:15:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:01:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T04:30:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:24:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T17:17:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-01T01:03:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-29T23:42:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-01T00:14:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-03T18:38:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-07T00:04:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents GraphMend, a compiler technique that automatically fixes FX graph breaks in PyTorch 2 programs. Although PyTorch 2 introduced TorchDynamo and TorchInductor to enable just-in-time graph compilation, certain code patterns still cause graph breaks that force execution to fall back to Python eager mode, introducing costly CPU-GPU synchronization and reducing optimization opportunities. Our investigation of 195 Hugging Face models reveals that 13.8% of models exhibit graph breaks. GraphMend automatically eliminates fixable breaks through source-level program analysis and transformations. It analyzes AST-level program structure to identify graph-break patterns and applies transformations only when their semantic preservation can be statically established. These transformations enable PyTorch to capture larger, uninterrupted FX graphs without manual refactoring by developers. We evaluate GraphMend on all 27 models found to exhibit graph breaks in our investigation. GraphMend eliminates 107 of 147 graph breaks (73%), fully fixing all breaks in 21 models. In our experiments on NVIDIA GPUs, GraphMend achieves up to 26x cold-start speedup, 5x on average, and up to 1.39x steady-state forward pass speedup. These results demonstrate that semantics-aware source-level analysis and transformation are effective complements to PyTorch's dynamic JIT compilation pipeline, substantially improving both usability and performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication at the International Symposium on Code Generation and Optimization (CGO 2027)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16248","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:43:55Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:43:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-07T02:15:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17154","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17154","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17154","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasamin","familyName":"Jalalian","name":"Jalalian, Yasamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mostafa","familyName":"Samir","name":"Samir, Mostafa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boumediene","familyName":"Hamzi","name":"Hamzi, Boumediene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peyman","familyName":"Tavallali","name":"Tavallali, Peyman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houman","familyName":"Owhadi","name":"Owhadi, Houman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data-efficient Kernel Methods for Learning Hamiltonian Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T16:50:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:59:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-03T14:21:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-04T00:58:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hamiltonian dynamics describe a wide range of physical systems. As such, data-driven simulations of Hamiltonian systems are important for many scientific and engineering problems. In this work, we propose kernel-based methods for identifying and forecasting Hamiltonian systems directly from trajectory data. We present two approaches: a 2-step method that reconstructs trajectories before learning the Hamiltonian, and a 1-step method that jointly infers both. Across several benchmark systems, including mass-spring dynamics, a nonlinear pendulum, and the Henon-Heiles system, we demonstrate that our framework achieves accurate, data-efficient predictions and outperforms 2-step kernel-based baselines, particularly in scarce-data regimes, while preserving the Hamiltonian structure. Moreover, we prove a priori error estimates, ensuring reliability of the learned models. We also provide a more general, problem-agnostic numerical framework that goes beyond Hamiltonian systems and can be used for data-driven learning of arbitrary dynamical systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17154","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:05:18Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:05:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-04T02:37:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16509","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16509","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16509","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haijin","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Haijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiezhang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jiezhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yurong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yurong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiangqiang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Qiangqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoqing","familyName":"Chao","name":"Chao, Guoqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongyong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yongyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyong","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jingyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SlowFast-SCI: Slow-Fast Deep Unfolding Learning for Spectral Compressive Imaging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T03:09:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:15:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-26T04:24:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-29T01:33:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T13:31:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:57:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Humans learn in two complementary ways: a slow, cumulative process that builds broad, general knowledge, and a fast, on-the-fly process that captures specific experiences. Existing deep-unfolding methods for spectral compressive imaging (SCI) mirror only the slow component-relying on heavy pre-training with many unfolding stages-yet they lack the rapid adaptation needed to handle new optical configurations. As a result, they falter on out-of-distribution cameras, especially in bespoke spectral setups unseen during training. This depth also incurs heavy computation and slow inference. To bridge this gap, we introduce SlowFast-SCI, a dual-speed framework seamlessly integrated into any deep unfolding network beyond SCI systems. During slow learning, we pre-train or reuse a priors-based backbone and distill it via imaging guidance into a compact fast-unfolding model. In the fast learning stage, lightweight adaptation modules are embedded within each stage and fine-turned self-supervised at test time via a self-supervised loss-without retraining the backbone. To the best of our knowledge, SlowFast-SCI is the first testtime adaptation-driven deep unfolding framework for efficient, self-adaptive spectral reconstruction. Its dual-stage design unites offline robustness with on-the-fly per-sample calibration-yielding over 70% reduction in parameters and FLOPs, up to 5.79 dB PSNR improvement on out-of-distribution data, preserved cross-domain adaptability, and a 4x faster adaptation speed. In addition, its modularity integrates with any deep-unfolding network, paving the way for self-adaptive, field-deployable imaging and expanded computational imaging modalities. Code is available in Supplementary Material. The models, datasets, and code are available at https://github.com/XuanLu11/SlowFast-SCI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16509","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:04Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:35:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17297","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17297","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17297","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanying","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hanying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James C.","familyName":"Massey","name":"Massey, James C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nedunchezhian","familyName":"Swaminathan","name":"Swaminathan, Nedunchezhian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JPResUnet: A Joint Probability Density Function Translation Model in Partially Premixed Flames"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T00:35:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:40:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.combustflame.2026.115233","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Machine learning (ML) models are often constrained by their limitations in extrapolation, which restricts their applicability in engineering contexts. Conversely, while exhibiting broad generality, many established scientific models seem to lack the necessary accuracy. This study addresses these challenges by introducing JPResUnet (Joint PDF Residual U-net), a novel model that integrates the strengths of both ML and traditional scientific approaches to predict sub-grid joint probability density functions (PDFs) in partially premixed flames. JPResUnet employs a residual U-Net architecture to translate classic $β$-PDFs to sub-grid PDFs. The model is trained using direct numerical simulation (DNS) data from methane-air moderate or intense low-oxygen dilution (MILD) combustion and is initially tested through a priori assessments on out-of-sample data. Comparative analyses against an artificial neural network (ANN) and the $β$-PDF approach demonstrate that JPResUnet consistently outperforms these methods in capturing complex sub-grid features with greater accuracy and robustness for both box and Gaussian kernels of varying widths, and for more extrapolated cases. Subsequent a posteriori assessment involves two versions of JPResUnet with different output PDF resolutions, which are deployed for large eddy simulation (LES) of a multi-regime burner through the look-up table (LUT) approach. The higher resolution model yields improvements in temperature estimates compared to the conventional LUT method. This highlights the potential of the JPResUnet model for robust and accurate LES of reacting flows with ML."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17297","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:08:39Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:08:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:23:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17106","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17106","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17106","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hendry M.","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Hendry M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Canonical quantization of a product within the Cahill-Glauber correspondence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T14:56:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:57:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-22T04:51:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:22:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-01T05:39:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-02T00:25:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-16T06:37:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:47:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The (canonical) quantization of a product of two phase-space functions can be expressed as a commutative mapping between the quantizations of its factors. In this note, we discuss such a mapping within the Cahill-Glauber $s$-parameterized correspondence framework, providing a heuristic (hence less mathematically rigorous, but arguably more easily understood) derivation for the bidifferential form. The term \"hatted star product\" is coined by virtue of the structural similarity to the star product in phase space, a special case of which is the celebrated Moyal star product."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages. Added more literature review. Improved clarity and scope of discussion. This is a technical note not planned for any journal submission"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17106","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:04:10Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:04:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:56:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17390","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17390","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17390","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junzhe","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Junzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyi","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Tiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zifan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"BoKui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, BoKui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guyue","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Guyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FGGS-LiDAR: Ultra-Fast, GPU-Accelerated Simulation from General 3DGS Models to LiDAR"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.6.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T40, 68U05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T06:52:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:13:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-19T13:02:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:49:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T10:39:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:44:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) has emerged as a strong representation for photorealistic rendering, its vast ecosystem of assets remains difficult to use for high-performance LiDAR simulation, a critical tool for robotics and autonomous driving. We present \\textbf{FGGS-LiDAR}, a geometry-first framework that bridges this gap in a plug-and-play manner. Our method converts pretrained 3DGS assets into watertight meshes directly from Gaussian parameters, without requiring LiDAR-specific supervision or architectural alterations, via volumetric discretization and Truncated Signed Distance Field (TSDF) extraction. We pair this with a GPU-accelerated ray-casting module that simulates LiDAR returns at over 500 FPS and supports batched multi-environment simulation with up to 4096 environments. In large-scale parallel settings, FGGS-LiDAR achieves an order-of-magnitude lower LiDAR simulation latency than Isaac Sim. We validate FGGS-LiDAR on both indoor and outdoor scenes, demonstrating high LiDAR-simulation fidelity. Furthermore, on COLMAP-posed indoor benchmarks, we compare against existing 3DGS-to-mesh baselines and report lower LiDAR-simulation error. Code is at https://github.com/discoverse-dev/FGGS-LiDAR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17390","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:10:51Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:10:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-05T02:29:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17878","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17878","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17878","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Hacker","name":"Hacker, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lilian","familyName":"Edwards","name":"Edwards, Lilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atoosa","familyName":"Kasirzadeh","name":"Kasirzadeh, Atoosa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI, Digital Platforms, and the New Systemic Risk"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T15:14:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:40:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-23T11:01:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T00:32:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As artificial intelligence (AI) becomes increasingly embedded in digital, social, and institutional infrastructures, and AI and platforms are merged into hybrid structures, systemic risk has emerged as a critical but undertheorized challenge. In this paper, we develop a rigorous framework for understanding systemic risk in AI, platform, and hybrid system governance, drawing on insights from finance, complex systems theory, climate change, and cybersecurity - domains where systemic risk has already shaped regulatory responses. We argue that recent legislation, including the EU's AI Act and Digital Services Act (DSA), invokes systemic risk but relies on narrow or ambiguous characterizations of this notion, sometimes reducing this risk to specific capabilities present in frontier AI models, or to harms occurring in economic market settings. The DSA, we show, actually does a better job at identifying systemic risk than the more recent AI Act. Our framework highlights novel risk pathways, including the possibility of systemic failures arising from the interaction of multiple AI agents. We identify four levels of AI-related systemic risk and emphasize that discrimination at scale and systematic hallucinations, despite their capacity to destabilize institutions and fundamental rights, may not fall under current legal definitions, given the AI Act's focus on frontier model capabilities. We then test the DSA, the AI Act, and our own framework on five key examples, and propose reforms that broaden systemic risk assessments, strengthen coordination between regulatory regimes, and explicitly incorporate collective harms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication at ACM FAccT (2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17878","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:22:23Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:22:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:03:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16931","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16931","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16931","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyu","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lv","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Lv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Equip Pre-ranking with Target Attention by Residual Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.0; I.5.0; I.7.0","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T05:33:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:46:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T09:26:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:37:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T11:51:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:45:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The pre-ranking stage in industrial recommendation systems faces a fundamental conflict between efficiency and effectiveness. While powerful models like Target Attention (TA) excel at capturing complex feature interactions in the ranking stage, their high computational cost makes them infeasible for pre-ranking, which often relies on simplistic vector-product models. This disparity creates a significant performance bottleneck for the entire system. To bridge this gap, we propose TARQ, a novel pre-ranking framework. Inspired by generative models, TARQ's key innovation is to equip pre-ranking with an architecture approximate to TA by Residual Quantization. This allows us to bring the modeling power of TA into the latency-critical pre-ranking stage for the first time, establishing a new state-of-the-art trade-off between accuracy and efficiency. Extensive offline experiments and large-scale online A/B tests at Taobao demonstrate TARQ's significant improvements in ranking performance. Consequently, our model has been fully deployed in production, serving tens of millions of daily active users and yielding substantial business improvements. The code and data are available at https://github.com/zyody/tarq_sigir2026."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 2 figures, accepted by SIGIR 2026 Short Paper Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16931","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:59:56Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:59:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:03:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17252","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17252","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17252","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Wisniewski","name":"Wisniewski, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Zdyrski","name":"Zdyrski, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Homophily and wealth inequality shape mitigation behavior in coupled social-climate models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"91A22 (Primary) 91D10 and 91B76 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T22:02:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:05:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-11T20:18:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-15T00:10:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/2632-072x/ae6009","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding the role of human behavior in shaping environmental outcomes is crucial for addressing global challenges such as climate change. Environmental systems are influenced not only by natural factors like temperature, but also by human decisions regarding mitigation efforts, which are often based on forecasts or predictions about future environmental conditions. Over time, different outcomes can emerge, including scenarios where the environment deteriorates despite efforts to mitigate, or where successful mitigation leads to environmental resilience. Additionally, fluctuations in the level of human participation in mitigation can occur, reflecting shifts in collective behavior. In this study, we consider a variety of human mitigation decisions, in addition to the feedback loop that is created by changes in human behavior because of environmental changes. While these outcomes are based on simplified models, they offer important insights into the dynamics of human decision-making and the factors that influence effective action in the context of environmental sustainability. This study aims to examine key social dynamics influencing society's response to a worsening climate. While others conclude that homophily prompts greater warming unconditionally, this model finds that homophily can prevent catastrophic effects given a poor initial environmental state. Assuming that poor countries have the resources to do so, a consensus in that class group to defect from the strategy of the rich group (who are generally incentivized to continue ``business as usual'') can frequently prevent the vegetation proportion from converging to 0."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Added figure varying homophily and population composition, changed title; 14 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17252","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:07:37Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:07:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:35:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17370","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17370","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17370","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stavros","familyName":"Melemenidis","name":"Melemenidis, Stavros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dixin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cody","familyName":"Jensen","name":"Jensen, Cody","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph B.","familyName":"Schulz","name":"Schulz, Joseph B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murat","familyName":"Surucu","name":"Surucu, Murat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amy S.","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Amy S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward E.","familyName":"Graves","name":"Graves, Edward E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengying","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Mengying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter G.","familyName":"Maxim","name":"Maxim, Peter G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Currell","name":"Currell, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Billy W.","familyName":"Loo","name":"Loo, Billy W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lawrie","familyName":"Skinner","name":"Skinner, Lawrie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. Ramish","familyName":"Ashraf","name":"Ashraf, M. Ramish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Non-invasive Reversible Software-based Electron FLASH Irradiation Configuration of a Linear Accelerator in Clinical Use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Medical Physics (physics.med-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T05:36:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:11:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T02:46:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:18:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-24T22:03:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-28T00:11:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Configuring clinical linear accelerators (linacs) for ultra-high dose rate (UHDR) electron experiments typically requires invasive hardware manipulation and/or irreversible manufacturer modifications, limiting broader implementation. We present an independently developed UHDR electron configuration of a clinical TrueBeam linac that allows reversible switching between preclinical UHDR and conventional (CONV) modes using only non-invasive software settings. UHDR mode was achieved via service mode software with RF and beam current settings typical of a photon beam, the photon target and monitor chamber retracted, and a clinically unused low-energy scattering foil inserted. An external AC current transformer (ACCT) for beam monitoring, anatomy-specific collimator, and sample holder were mounted on the accessory tray, with external ion chamber in solid water for exit dose monitoring. Percent depth dose (PDD) was measured for UHDR and CONV beams. Dose-per-pulse (DPP) was varied by adjusting gun voltage and quantified with radiochromic film at different source-to-surface distances (SSD). Beam profiles assessed dose uniformity and usable field size. Dose calibration was established between film, ACCT, and ion chamber, and day-to-day reproducibility was tested. PDD confirmed similar energies for UHDR (12.8MeV) and CONV (11.9MeV) beams with matching profiles through mouse thickness. Maximum DPP exceeded 0.5Gy, reaching ~1.5Gy for collimated in vivo setups and ~0.7Gy at extended SSD for tissue culture. Field flatness and symmetry were maintained, supporting organ-specific irradiations and up to 5cm fields for culture. Calibration showed strong linearity across detectors, and output variation was \u0026lt;4%. We demonstrated accurate, reproducible UHDR delivery on a widely available clinical linac with no invasive hardware manipulation, enabling preclinical FLASH research on a clinical treatment machine."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 4 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17370","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:10:23Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:10:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-28T04:04:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16621","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16621","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16621","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hiun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tae Kwan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Tae Kwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeryun","familyName":"Won","name":"Won, Taeryun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Role of Vocabularies in Learning Sparse Representations for Ranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T10:44:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:26:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-19T12:32:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:10:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learned Sparse Retrieval (LSR) such as SPLADE has growing interest for effective semantic 1st stage matching while enjoying the efficiency of inverted indices. A recent work on learning SPLADE models with expanded vocabularies (ESPLADE) was proposed to represent queries and documents into a sparse space of custom vocabulary which have different levels of vocabularic granularity. Within this effort, however, there have not been many studies on the role of vocabulary in SPLADE models and their relationship to retrieval efficiency and effectiveness.\n To study this, we construct BERT models with 100K-sized output vocabularies, one initialized with the ESPLADE pretraining method and one initialized randomly. After finetune on real-world search click logs, we applied logit score-based queries and documents pruning to max size for further balancing efficiency. The experimental result in our evaluation set shows that, when pruning is applied, the two models are effective compared to the 32K-sized normal SPLADE model in the computational budget under the BM25. And the ESPLADE models are more effective than the random vocab model, while having a similar retrieval cost.\n The result indicates that the size and pretrained weight of output vocabularies play the role of configuring the representational specification for queries, documents, and their interactions in the retrieval engine, beyond their original meaning and purposes in NLP. These findings can provide a new room for improvement for LSR by identifying the importance of representational specification from vocabulary configuration for efficient and effective retrieval."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"fix citation style; add some previous work description at the beginning of section 3;"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16621","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:52:41Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:52:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:49:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17247","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17247","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17247","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongheon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dongheon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younghoo","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Younghoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jung-Woo","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Jung-Woo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepASA: An Object-Oriented Multi-Purpose Network for Auditory Scene Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T21:44:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:05:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-01T08:47:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:39:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-23T09:37:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:29:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose DeepASA, a multi-purpose model for auditory scene analysis that performs multi-input multi-output (MIMO) source separation, dereverberation, sound event detection (SED), audio classification, and direction-of-arrival estimation (DoAE) within a unified framework. DeepASA is designed for complex auditory scenes where multiple, often similar, sound sources overlap in time and move dynamically in space. To achieve robust and consistent inference across tasks, we introduce an object-oriented processing (OOP) strategy. This approach encapsulates diverse auditory features into object-centric representations and refines them through a chain-of-inference (CoI) mechanism. The pipeline comprises a dynamic temporal kernel-based feature extractor, a transformer-based aggregator, and an object separator that yields per-object features. These features feed into multiple task-specific decoders. Our object-centric representations naturally resolve the parameter association ambiguity inherent in traditional track-wise processing. However, early-stage object separation can lead to failure in downstream ASA tasks. To address this, we implement temporal coherence matching (TCM) within the chain-of-inference, enabling multi-task fusion and iterative refinement of object features using estimated auditory parameters. We evaluate DeepASA on representative spatial audio benchmark datasets, including ASA2, MC-FUSS, and STARSS23. Experimental results show that our model achieves state-of-the-art performance across all evaluated tasks, demonstrating its effectiveness in both source separation and auditory parameter estimation under diverse spatial auditory scenes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 13 figures, 11 tables, published in NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17247","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:07:30Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:07:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16896","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16896","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16896","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shing-Tung","familyName":"Yau","name":"Yau, Shing-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Shuai","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Yi-Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Improved Yau-Yau Algorithm for High Dimensional Nonlinear Filtering Problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"93E11, 60G35, 62M20, 35K15, 60H15, 65N15, 65M12, 68Q25","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T03:13:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-08T00:25:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.4310/pamq.251222233358","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Nonlinear state estimation under noisy observations is rapidly intractable as system dimension increases. We introduce an improved Yau-Yau filtering framework that breaks the curse of dimensionality and extends real-time nonlinear filtering to systems with up to thousands of state dimensions, achieving high-accuracy estimates in just a few seconds with rigorous theoretical error guarantees. This new approach integrates quasi-Monte Carlo low-discrepancy sampling, a novel offline-online update, high-order multi-scale kernel approximations, fully log-domain likelihood computation, and a local resampling-restart mechanism, all realized with CPU/GPU-parallel computation. Theoretical analysis guarantees local truncation error \\(O(Δt^2 + D^*(n))\\) and global error \\(O(Δt + D^*(n)/Δt)\\), where \\(Δt\\) is the time step and \\(D^*(n)\\) the star-discrepancy. Numerical experiments, spanning large-scale nonlinear cubic sensors up to 1000 dimensions, highly nonlinear small-scale problems, and linear Gaussian benchmarks, demonstrate sub-quadratic runtime scaling, sub-linear error growth, and excellent performance that surpasses the extended and unscented Kalman filters (EKF, UKF) and the particle filter (PF) under strong nonlinearity, while matching or exceeding the optimal Kalman-Bucy filter in linear regimes. By breaking the curse of dimensionality, our method enables accurate, real-time, high-dimensional nonlinear filtering, opening broad opportunities for applications in science and engineering."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16896","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:59:08Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:59:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-08T02:30:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrtash","familyName":"Harandi","name":"Harandi, Mehrtash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dinh","familyName":"Phung","name":"Phung, Dinh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trung","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Trung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unveiling m-Sharpness Through the Structure of Stochastic Gradient Noise"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:40:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:46:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T14:49:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:48:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T06:16:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-16T01:23:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-05T11:48:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:42:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-02T05:41:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-03T00:27:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sharpness-aware minimization (SAM) has emerged as a highly effective technique to improve model generalization, but its underlying principles are not fully understood. We investigate m-sharpness, where SAM performance improves monotonically as the micro-batch size for computing perturbations decreases, a phenomenon critical for distributed training yet lacking rigorous explanation. We leverage an extended Stochastic Differential Equation (SDE) framework and analyze stochastic gradient noise (SGN) to characterize the dynamics of SAM variants, including n-SAM and m-SAM. Our analysis reveals that stochastic perturbations induce an implicit variance-based sharpness regularization whose strength increases as m decreases. Motivated by this insight, we propose Reweighted SAM (RW-SAM), which employs sharpness-weighted sampling to mimic the generalization benefits of m-SAM while remaining parallelizable. Comprehensive experiments validate our theory and method.Code is available at https://github.com/RitianLuo/RW-SAM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025; added code availability"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:25:17Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:25:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-03T02:14:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17180","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17180","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17180","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Arbour","name":"Arbour, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh","familyName":"Parikh","name":"Parikh, Harsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bijan","familyName":"Niknam","name":"Niknam, Bijan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elizabeth","familyName":"Stuart","name":"Stuart, Elizabeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kara","familyName":"Rudolph","name":"Rudolph, Kara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avi","familyName":"Feller","name":"Feller, Avi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Regularizing Extrapolation in Causal Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T18:05:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:01:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T01:37:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:17:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:47:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:17:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many common estimators in machine learning and causal inference are linear smoothers, where the prediction is a weighted average of the training outcomes. Some estimators, such as ordinary least squares and kernel ridge regression, allow for arbitrarily negative weights, which improve feature imbalance but often at the cost of increased dependence on parametric modeling assumptions and higher variance. By contrast, estimators like importance weighting and random forests (sometimes implicitly) restrict weights to be non-negative, reducing dependence on parametric modeling and variance at the cost of worse imbalance. In this paper, we propose a unified framework that directly penalizes the level of extrapolation, replacing the current practice of a hard non-negativity constraint with a soft constraint and corresponding hyperparameter. We derive a worst-case extrapolation error bound and introduce a novel \"bias-bias-variance\" tradeoff, encompassing biases due to feature imbalance, model misspecification, and estimator variance; this tradeoff is especially pronounced in high dimensions, particularly when positivity is poor. We then develop an optimization procedure that regularizes this bound while minimizing imbalance and outline how to use this approach as a sensitivity analysis for dependence on parametric modeling assumptions. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach through synthetic experiments and a real-world application, involving the generalization of randomized controlled trial estimates to a target population of interest."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17180","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:05:55Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:05:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-02T02:27:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16652","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16652","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16652","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sota","familyName":"Hanai","name":"Hanai, Sota","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Anomalous Dynamical Screening of Relativistic Plasma in a Magnetic Field"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T11:57:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:28:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-24T08:16:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:41:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/ptep/ptag023","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study collective excitations in a relativistic collisionless plasma composed of massless fermions subject to an external magnetic field. We include dynamical electromagnetism and fluctuations of the chiral charge, while maintaining a vanishing net chiral charge. Within the framework of chiral kinetic theory, we find that the chiral anomaly gives a correction to the transverse photon self-energy and that a novel type of dynamical screening can emerge. In the strong magnetic field limit, we also show that the collective mode has a gap using the lowest Landau level approximation. We further discuss implications of the anomalous dynamical screening for neutron star phenomenology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages; v2: published version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16652","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:53:25Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:53:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-25T02:31:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17340","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17340","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17340","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longbin","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Longbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AERO-MPPI: Anchor-Guided Ensemble Trajectory Optimization for Agile Mapless Drone Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T03:21:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:10:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-21T09:10:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T00:35:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agile mapless navigation in cluttered 3D environments poses significant challenges for autonomous drones. Conventional mapping-planning-control pipelines incur high computational cost and propagate estimation errors. We present AERO-MPPI, a fully GPU-accelerated framework that unifies perception and planning through an anchor-guided ensemble of Model Predictive Path Integral (MPPI) optimizers. Specifically, we design a multi-resolution LiDAR point-cloud representation that rapidly extracts spatially distributed \"anchors\" as look-ahead intermediate endpoints, from which we construct polynomial trajectory guides to explore distinct homotopy path classes. At each planning step, we run multiple MPPI instances in parallel and evaluate them with a two-stage multi-objective cost that balances collision avoidance and goal reaching. Implemented entirely with NVIDIA Warp GPU kernels, AERO-MPPI achieves real-time onboard operation and mitigates the local-minima failures of single-MPPI approaches. Extensive simulations in forests, verticals, and inclines demonstrate sustained reliable flight above 7 m/s, with success rates above 80% and smoother trajectories compared to state-of-the-art baselines. Real-world experiments on a LiDAR-equipped quadrotor with NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX 16G confirm that AERO-MPPI runs in real time onboard and consistently achieves safe, agile, and robust flight in complex cluttered environments. Code is available at https://github.com/XinChen-stars/AERO_MPPI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICRA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17340","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:09:40Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:09:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:15:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16763","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16763","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16763","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajay","familyName":"Jha","name":"Jha, Ajay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alvaro","familyName":"Palomino","name":"Palomino, Alvaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stéphane","familyName":"Auffret","name":"Auffret, Stéphane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hélène","familyName":"Béa","name":"Béa, Hélène","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo C.","familyName":"Sousa","name":"Sousa, Ricardo C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liliana D.","familyName":"Buda-Prejbeanu","name":"Buda-Prejbeanu, Liliana D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernard","familyName":"Dieny","name":"Dieny, Bernard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Low-Noise Nanoscale Vortex Sensor for Out-of-Plane Magnetic Field Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T18:06:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:53:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1021/acsnano.5c14180","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study investigates a vortex sensor based on a nanoscale (sub-100 nm) magnetic tunnel junction (MTJ) with a strong shape anisotropy, designed for sensitivity to the out-of-plane magnetic field component ($H_z$). The sensor comprises a free layer with a vortex configuration and a perpendicularly magnetized reference layer, which provides a reproducible and linear response when excited by a perpendicular magnetic field. Experimental measurements and micromagnetic simulations were combined to systematically assess the influence of structural parameters, specifically aspect ratio and defect landscape, on key sensor performance metrics, including dynamic range, sensitivity, and detectivity. The out-of-plane vortex sensor demonstrates a significantly improved dynamic range exceeding 200 mT, compared to the 40-80 mT typical of conventional in-plane vortex sensors. Frequency-dependent noise measurements reveal that the sensor exhibits low intrinsic noise, along with improved detectivity and resolution. This performance is ascribed to the field-dependent expansion and contraction of the vortex core, which reduces Barkhausen-type noise caused by defect-induced pinning potentials. Moreover, the sub-100\\,nm lateral dimensions of the sensor enable scalable array integration, providing further enhancements in noise and detectivity through collective averaging. These results underscore the potential of this sensor architecture for advanced magnetic field sensing applications requiring a wide dynamic range and high measurement accuracy at the same time."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16763","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:56:02Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:56:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:49:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17529","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17529","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17529","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trinh","familyName":"Tuan","name":"Tuan, Trinh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A refined variant of Hartley convolution: algebraic structures, spectral radius and related issues"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Classical Analysis and ODEs (math.CA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"46J10, 47A10, 44A35, 47A30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T08:51:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:20:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T06:58:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:28:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-18T03:05:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-19T01:18:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1080/10652469.2026.2626960","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we propose a novel convolution product associated with the $\\mathscr{H}$-transform, denoted by $\\underset{\\mathscr{H}}{\\ast}$, and explore its fundamental properties. Here, the $\\mathscr{H}$-transform may be regarded as a refined variant of the classical Fourier, Hartley transform, with kernel function depending on two parameters $a,b$. Our first contribution shows that the space of integrable functions, equipped with multiplication given by the $\\underset{\\mathscr{H}}{\\ast}$-convolution, constitutes the commutative Banach algebra over the complex field, albeit without an identity element. Second, establishes the Wiener--Lévy type invertibility criterion for $\\mathscr{H}$-algebras, obtained through the density property and process of unitarization, which serves as a key step toward the proof of Gelfand's spectral radius theorem. Third, provides an explicit upper-bound of Young's inequality for $\\underset{\\mathscr{H}}{\\ast}$-convolution and its direct corollary. Finally, all of these theoretical findings are applied to analyze specific classes of the Fredholm integral equations and heat source problems, yielding a priori estimates under the established assumptions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, accepted by Integral Transforms Spec. Funct"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17529","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:14:09Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:14:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-19T03:01:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17173","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17173","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17173","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tahereh Sadat","familyName":"Parvini","name":"Parvini, Tahereh Sadat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bilayer Cuprate Antiferromagnets Enable Programmable Cavity Optomagnonics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T17:38:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:00:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T20:02:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:05:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hybrid platforms that couple microwave photons to collective spin excitations offer promising routes for coherent information processing, yet conventional magnets face inherent trade-offs among coupling strength, coherence, and tunability. We demonstrate that bilayer cuprate antiferromagnets, exemplified by YBa2Cu3O6+x, provide an alternative approach enabled by their unique magnon spectrum. Using a neutron-constrained bilayer spin model, we obtain the complete Gamma-point spectrum and identify an in-plane acoustic alpha mode that remains gapless and Zeeman-linear, alongside an in-plane optical beta mode stabilized by weak anisotropy whose frequency can be tuned from the gigahertz to terahertz range. When coupled to a single-mode microwave cavity, these modes create two distinct channels with a magnetically tunable alpha-photon interaction and a nearly field-independent beta-photon interaction. This asymmetric behavior enables continuous, single-parameter control spanning from dispersive to strong coupling regimes. In the dispersive limit, our analysis reveals cavity-mediated magnon-magnon coupling, while near triple resonance the normal modes reorganize into bright and dark superpositions governed by a single collective energy scale. The calculated transmission exhibits vacuum-Rabi splittings, dispersive shifts, and Fano-like lineshapes that provide concrete experimental benchmarks and suggest potential for programmable filtering and coherent state transfer across the gigahertz-terahertz frequency range if realized experimentally with suitable interfaces."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17173","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:05:45Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:05:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-11T03:28:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":96,"totalPages":4,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-22T16%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202025-09-23T15%